{"t":1788755979,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"ba48452f-2739-4c3f-a85b-d21bceabf1ff","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-07T01:11:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 070111\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n911 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nClosely monitoring the threat of tidal flooding at Annapolis due\nto prolonged onshore flow. Coastal Flood Advisories may be\nneeded shortly.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry and seasonable conditions, with lower humidity through\n  early portions of the week.\n\n- 2) Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable conditions, with lower\nhumidity through early portions of the week.\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure currently stretches across\nthe Great Lakes. Net northerly winds on the eastern side of this\nanticyclone is supporting dry conditions over the area. The 00Z\nIAD sounding depicted a bit of saturation around 800-750 mb\nwhich was likely associated with the lingering mid-level\nclouds. Otherwise, the profile is rather dry, and will remain\nthat way through Monday. This will maintain a lower dew point\nair mass into the Labor Day holiday.\n\nDaily high temperatures through Monday will be in the low 80s,\naccompanied by overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid- \nAtlantic on Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the\nsoutheast CONUS. Temperatures likely begin to creep up again \nduring this time, accompanied by increasing humidity as mid-\nlevel dry air tracks south of the region with more humid air \ncoming in from the north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid week.\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Appalachians starts to flatten into \nzonal flow beginning Wednesday. As this occurs, a trough forming \nover Quebec will begin to track east across the northeast CONUS, \nwith a stemming cold front moving across the East Coast by Thursday. \nTemperatures gradually increase during this time, along with \nincreasing humidity ahead of the front. By Thursday, temps \npotentially reach the upper 80s to low 90s. \n\nAs this frontal boundary tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, showers\n& thunderstorms are possible across the region, beginning Wednesday\nevening and into Thursday. The 12z guidance remains unclear in \nterms of timing and the exact evolution of this system, so a lot\nof uncertainties remain. There is general agreement in trough\ndigging south into the region, but the exact strength and \ntiming of that trough is still being ironed out. Temperatures \nThursday are expected to be in the 80s to near 90, with dew \npoint in the 60s to perhaps low 70s. This should yield plenty of\nCAPE south of the front itself and allow for at least some \nthunderstorm development if the forcing arrives near peak \nheating. The severe threat is still in question, but remains \nnon-zero. If the trough tracks far enough south, there could be \na fair amount of wind shear to work with as well, which would \nincrease that severe threat. Some of the AI guidance \n(particularly the WxNext) do highlight our region, so it is \ncertainly something to keep a close eye on. Will continue to \nmonitor latest guidance in hopes of honing in on a more \nconfident solution. \n\nAs this cold front transitions offshore towards the end of next \nweek, temps drop again & conditions become much drier heading into \nthe weekend as high pressure moves in overhead.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nVFR conditions should persist through Wednesday evening given\nthe general influence of high pressure over, or near the region.\nThis dome of high pressure does depart by Tuesday which yields a\nreturn of south to southwesterly flow. \n\nShowers & thunderstorms associated with an incoming cold front \ncould temporarily bring sub-VFR conditions to terminals by\nThursday afternoon. Ahead of the front on Thursday, winds could\ngust between 20-25 knots before diminishing overnight.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through the holiday weekend. \nGenerally light flow is expected, although gusts could creep up\nto 10 to 15 knots at times.\n\nNortheasterly winds Monday shift southerly on Tuesday, \nremaining below SCA levels.\n\nSCAs are possible across the Bay by Wednesday evening ahead of \nan incoming cold front. Winds could remain elevated throughout \nThursday as this frontal boundary tracks east across the coast.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies remain elevated over the next several days due\nto an onshore flow. The current forecast would carry Annapolis\ninto minor tidal flooding during the next couple late night high\ntides. Depending on how the tidal anomalies change in the next\nhour, Coastal Flood Advisories may be needed. At other sensitive\nlocations, some places could push into Action.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/CJL/CPB\nAVIATION...CJL/CPB\nMARINE...CJL/CPB\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788755928,"days":{"2026-09-07":{"hi":84,"lo":63,"pop":5,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":86,"lo":66,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":89,"lo":73,"pop":3,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-10":{"hi":91,"lo":69,"pop":54,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-11":{"hi":82,"lo":65,"pop":17,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":86,"lo":68,"pop":23,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-13":{"hi":88,"lo":null,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788753600,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,9900,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null